{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/selective-propagation-delta-discipline.md", "content": "# Selective propagation: avoid overfitting to a local gap

For grids where background cells selectively change to a third color, do not infer the rule from one visually salient adjacency (for example, two same-color markers with one background cell between them). In the task-0c08c3f2 interaction, two submissions based on local interpolation and broader hand-guessed propagation were incorrect. The supplied demos displayed much wider, selective regions of background-to-3 changes and also a no-op case with a lone non-background pixel; these observations do not validate either attempted test output.

For future recurrences, record exact input/output deltas for every demo, including unchanged markers and unchanged background regions. Compare explicit candidates such as row/column rays, marker-bounded intervals, and other structured propagation against all changed and unchanged examples. Keep these as hypotheses until one explains every demo. After an incorrect submission, discard the full proposed rule rather than making local tweaks. Use further demos to resolve named uncertainties, and preserve the benchmark's exact one-action JSON response contract.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u032/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/selective-propagation-delta-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/sparse-recoloring-component-analysis.md", "content": "## Sparse recoloring amid unrelated objects

This task class showed multiple demos with many colors and disconnected objects but only a small, consistent subset recolored. The useful transferable method is to inventory 4-connected components by color, recording size, bounding box, normalized shape, and changed/unchanged status. Compare transformed components across demonstrations, and use unchanged lookalikes as negative evidence. Test candidate triggers (shape identity, component size, relation/alignment to another object) against every demo's exact input/output delta before applying to the test.

Do not infer the destination-color mapping from proximity alone or from one visually salient example. Verify whether the transformation applies to a complete connected component or only matching cells/subcomponents. When two explanations remain viable, request a discriminating demo rather than submitting a speculative no-op or arbitrary recoloring. The session's hypotheses did not resolve the rule, so this note records only the validated analysis discipline—not a guessed transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u032/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/sparse-recoloring-component-analysis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/outline-cavity-filling.md", "content": "# Outline cavities: recognize the bounded region

A valid demonstration showed color 1 outlining rectangular rooms against color 5. Only the interior background cells were recolored to 4; the outline and all exterior cells remained unchanged. One demo had a 2x4 outline with a 2x2 interior; another had multiple outline-bounded rectangles, where only each actual cavity's interior changed. A later test used color 8 outlines against background 2, with a 4-row by 5-column rectangle bounded on its left by a vertical segment, top and bottom by horizontal segments, and right edge by the grid border: fill the enclosed 3x3 interior, not all cells of the right-side background component.

Procedure: derive the exact delta first; map continuous line segments into closed rectangular boundaries (including the grid edge where it completes the boundary); fill only cells strictly inside, replacing background with 4. Preserve boundary pixels and unrelated background. Prior failed guesses included whole-grid fill, filling broad outer areas, and filling a nearby rectangular-looking patch without respecting the completed boundary. In particular, flood-fill connectivity alone can mislead when outlines partition the grid or reach its edge.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u032/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/outline-cavity-filling.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/demo-budget-and-turn-discipline.md", "content": "# Demo-budget and turn discipline

In interactive ARC streams with one action per turn, every tool/action must earn its turn. A demo request is useful only when it separates explicit, competing hypotheses; after a demo arrives, parse its actual pair before requesting another. Do not emit empty scratch/tool calls or request demonstrations reflexively without stating internally what prediction they will distinguish. Keep the output contract in view: exactly one valid action JSON object on the final line.

The session that motivated this note used repeated demos but did not compare the returned pairs concretely before requesting further examples, and included non-informative tool calls. This is a process correction, not a task-specific transformation rule. Use the demo only to update hypotheses; once enough evidence exists, compute and validate the proposed output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u032/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/demo-budget-and-turn-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-interaction-discipline", "file": "references/outline-color-scope.md", "content": "# Scope validation for outline-filling tasks

A demonstrated fill establishes only that the particular enclosed interior in that pair changes to the observed color. In a fresh grid with multiple closed outlines, especially outlines of different colors or dimensions, do not extrapolate that every cavity is selected. Inventory each cavity and compare boundary color, geometry, and interior against the demonstrated case. Preserve the border and exterior, and change only interiors supported by the rule. If the available demo does not distinguish candidate selectors (for example, one eligible-color outline versus another-color outline), spend a demo request on that ambiguity before submitting.

This lesson arose in a task-67c59573 recurrence: one demo showed a color-6 rectangular outline's background interior becoming 7; the test also included color-5 rectangular outlines. Two submissions filled both types, with differing scope, and were incorrect. The demos did not establish that the color-5 cavities should be filled, so this is a warning about overgeneralization, not a solved selection rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u032/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-interaction-discipline/references/outline-color-scope.md"}

